=head1 NAME

afsio_write - Write, append, or create files in AFS

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=for html
<div class="synopsis">

B<afsio write> S<<< B<-file> <I<AFS-filename>> >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    [B<-md5>] [B<-force>] S<<< [B<-synthesize> <I<length>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

B<afsio fidwrite> S<<< B<-vnode> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>> >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    [B<-md5>] [B<-force>] S<<< [B<-synthesize> <I<length>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

B<afsio append> S<<< B<-file> <I<AFS-filename>> >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    S<<< [B<-synthesize> <I<length>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

B<afsio fidappend> S<<< B<-vnode> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>> >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    S<<< [B<-synthesize> <I<length>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

=for html
</div>

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The B<afsio write> command reads data from standard input and writes it
to a new file in AFS at the path specified by the B<-file> argument.
The parent directory must already exist. If the file already exists, the
command exits with an error unless the B<-force> flag is provided.

The B<afsio fidwrite> command performs the same operation, but identifies
the target file by its FID (the standard C<volume.vnode.uniquifier> triple)
specified in the B<-vnode> argument. This file must already exist;
B<afsio fidwrite> cannot create new files. However, B<afsio fidwrite> can
operate on existing files without B<-force> if the file is empty, unlike
B<afsio write>.

The B<afsio append> command reads data from standard input and appends
it to an existing file in AFS. The file must already exist; if it does
not, the command exits.

The B<afsio fidappend> command performs the same operation as B<afsio
append>, but identifies the target file by its FID specified in the B<-vnode>
argument.

=head1 CAUTIONS

The maximum input size is 64 MB, and it is also the maximum size for the
B<-synthesize> option. Behavior with larger sizes is undefined.

The B<-force> flag for B<afsio write> and B<afsio fidwrite> silently
overwrites existing file data.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<-file> <I<filename>>

Specifies the full AFS pathname of the file to write or append to.
This option is used by B<afsio write> and B<afsio append>. A path
not beginning with F</afs> is interpreted relative to the cell's F<root.cell>.
A path beginning with F</> but not F</afs> is invalid.

=item B<-vnode> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>>

Specifies the file to write or append to as a FID triple (e.g.,
C<536870915.1.1>). This option is used by B<afsio fidwrite> and B<afsio
fidappend>.

=item B<-cell> <I<cell name>>

Specifies the AFS cell to operate in.

=item B<-realm> <I<realm name>>

Specifies the Kerberos realm to use for authentication.

=item B<-synthesize> <I<length>>

Instead of reading from standard input, generates synthetic data of the
specified length in bytes. The synthetic data contains offset markers
at 4096-byte intervals. This may be used for testing write performance
and verifying file server operations.

=item B<-md5>

Computes and prints an MD5 digest of the data written.

=item B<-force>

For B<afsio write>: allows overwriting an existing file. Without this
flag, the command exits with an error if the target file already exists.

For B<afsio fidwrite>: allows overwriting a file that already has
non-zero length.

This option has no effect on the B<append> and B<fidappend> commands.

=item B<-verbose>

Enables verbose output.

=item B<-clear>

Uses unencrypted (cleartext) connections to file servers.

=item B<-crypt>

Uses encrypted connections to file servers.

=item B<-as-user> <I<username>>

Uses localauth credentials to pretend to be the given user, allowing
authenticated RXAFS operations without a running kernel client.

=item B<-help>

Prints the online help for this command. All other valid options are
ignored.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

The following writes input into a file in AFS:

   % echo "Hello" | afsio write \
       -file /afs/testcell.example.com/greeting.txt
   % afsio read -file /afs/testcell.example.com/greeting.txt
   Hello

The following appends data to an existing file:

   % echo "Hello" | afsio write \
       -file /afs/testcell.example.com/greeting2.txt
   % echo "Hello Again" | afsio append \
       -file /afs/testcell.example.com/greeting2.txt
   % afsio read -file /afs/testcell.example.com/greeting2.txt
   Hello
   Hello Again

The following writes data to a file identified by its FID:

   % echo "Hello" | afsio fidwrite -vnode 536870918.4.2

The following command writes 1 MB of synthetic data to a file, computing
a MD5 digest and printing verbosely:

   % afsio write -file /afs/testcell.example.com/test.txt \
       -synthesize 1048576 -md5 -verbose

=head1 PRIVILEGE REQUIRED

The issuer must have the C<i> (insert) and C<l> (lookup) permissions on
the parent directory's ACL to create a new file. Overwriting or appending
to an existing file requires the C<w> (write) and C<l> (lookup)
permissions.

The B<-as-user> option requires access to the local key material, which
is typically only readable by the local superuser C<root> on a server
machine.

